<!--Post: 953--><!--Time: 1093911886-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST953"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, August 30, 2004 - 5:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;m not too sophistimacated when it comes to poetry. &#40;LOL&#41; However, even to a novice like me, BTK&#39;s poetry doesn&#39;t look too impressive. Of course, even the stuff that is highly regarded puts me to sleep... <BR> <BR>Anyway, this thread is for general comments about BTK&#39;s poetry.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 953-->
<!--Post: 921--><!--Time: 1093588454-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST921"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:zodiacfiles@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, August 26, 2004 - 11:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->25 years later, they&#39;ll be lucky to get a good tip.  They should have released the poem within days of receiving it, not decades; whoever might have known something then might have forgotten it, might not connect it now, or might even be dead.  WPD isn&#39;t doing anyone any favors.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 921-->
<!--Post: 922--><!--Time: 1093612664-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST922"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beckyks<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beckyks<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, August 27, 2004 - 6:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Seems strange that they have said all this time that they believed BTK was actually after the daughter - when the poem is clearly directed at the 63-year old Anna. <BR> <BR>I wonder what the purpose of the deception was?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 922-->
<!--Post: 934--><!--Time: 1093641289-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST934"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, August 27, 2004 - 2:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I do not think that the poem was original to BTK. despite WPD&#39;s belief that it was. I think that he borrows. It has the phrasings of Ovid and some of the very early erotic poets. Could BTK have the ability to be so eloquent in his phraseology and can then only end the poem &#39;why didn&#39;t you appear&#39;? Surely, he can do better than that. The poem is awkward at times.  He may daydream and toy with some of the feelings expressed, but I do not think him capable of feeling those things. He could not kill if he was truly capable of such emotions of the heart. <BR> <BR>And the younger woman may have still been the intended victim, with BTK trying again to confuse the issue and the police.  They are clearly grasping at straws: waiting a quarter of a century to develop a lead!!!!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 934-->
<!--Post: 940--><!--Time: 1093712588-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST940"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Micaela<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Micaela<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 28, 2004 - 10:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe this is not a communication from BTK. It is a &#34;fake&#34; genereated by LE to bait or flame BTK. It will be intersting to see if it is real.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 940-->
<!--Post: 943--><!--Time: 1093727575-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST943"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sapper<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sapper<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 28, 2004 - 2:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe the BTK is alot smarter then any of us are giving him credit for.The why he copies others then changes it around is a small window into his mind. If you dont want to be caught you make it look like its not your work. We realy need to step back and take a fresh lookk at it all. No serial killer just stops cold he was some where else killing. What other serial killers match his Mo in North America? <BR>I think he masked himself in this poem &#34;Oh Anna &#34; Just  to mess with WPD. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 943-->
<!--Post: 947--><!--Time: 1093749596-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST947"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:btk@mikecole.org" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_cole<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_cole<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 28, 2004 - 8:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Oh WPD Why Didn&#39;t You Release...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 947-->
<!--Post: 950--><!--Time: 1093804975-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST950"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sean<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sean<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, August 29, 2004 - 11:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The words that struck me from the poem were &#34;Memory of mirrors&#34; which I thought unique by itself. However there are a couple of references to this same phrase around the web. <BR>Including a poem called &#34;Memory of mirrors&#34; and a quote that a &#34;memory of mirrors helped me endure&#34;.Perhaps it was not an unknown phrase and therefore must have an origin somewhere.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 950-->
<!--Post: 954--><!--Time: 1093924236-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST954"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, August 30, 2004 - 8:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I wonder what poem BTK borrowed the lyrics to the &#34;Oh, Anna&#34; poem from? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 954-->
<!--Post: 955--><!--Time: 1093938182-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST955"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, August 31, 2004 - 12:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;I&#39;ll tie your hands till you can&#39;t make a stand.&#34;  <BR> <BR>To me, that&#39;s &#40;by far&#41; the most interesting line of BTK&#39;s adaptation of the &#34;Oh! Death&#34; poem/song. The rest of BTK&#39;s version at least rhymes. What&#39;s the deal? Compare below:  <BR> <BR>&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;  <BR> <BR>Oh, Death  <BR> <BR>BTK version from 1978:  <BR> <BR>Oh! Death to Nancy  <BR>What is this taht &#40;sic&#41; I can see  <BR>Cold icy hands taking hold of me  <BR>for Death has come, you all can see.  <BR>Hell has open it,s &#40;sic&#41; gate to trick me.  <BR>Oh! Death, Oh! Death, can&#39;t you spare  <BR>me, over for another year!  <BR>I&#39;ll stuff your jaws till you can&#39;t talk  <BR>I&#39;ll blind &#40;sic&#41; your leg&#39;s &#40;sic&#41; till you can&#39;t walk  <BR>I&#39;ll tie your hands till you can&#39;t make a  <BR>stand.  <BR>And finally I&#39;ll close your eyes so you  <BR>can&#39;t see  <BR>I&#39;ll bring sexual death unto you for me.  <BR>B.T.K.  <BR> <BR>The original lyrics:  <BR>&#40;The Anglin brothers were the first American group to record it in 1938&#41;  <BR> <BR>Oh Death  <BR>Oh, death Oh, death Won&#39;t you spare me over til another year  <BR>Well what is this that I can&#39;t see  <BR>With ice cold hands taking hold of me  <BR>Well I am death none can excel  <BR>I&#39;ll open the door to heaven or hell  <BR>Whoa death someone would pray  <BR>Could you wait to call me til another day  <BR>The children pray the preacher preached  <BR>Time and mercy is out of your reach  <BR>I&#39;ll fix your feet til you can&#39;t walk  <BR>I&#39;ll lock your jaw til you can&#39;t talk  <BR>I&#39;ll close your eyes so you can&#39;t see  <BR>This very hour come and go with me  <BR>In death I come to take the soul  <BR>Leave the body and leave it cold  <BR>To drop the flesh off of the frame  <BR>The earth and worms both have a claim  <BR>Oh death  <BR>Oh death  <BR>Won&#39;t you spare me over til another year  <BR>My mother came to my bed  <BR>Place a cold towel upon my head  <BR>My head is warm my feet are cold  <BR>Death is a movin upon my soul  <BR>Oh death how you&#39;re treatin me  <BR>You close my eyes so I can&#39;t see  <BR>Well you&#39;re hurtin my body you make me cold  <BR>You run my life right out of my soul  <BR>Oh death please consider my age  <BR>Please don&#39;t take me at this stage  <BR>My wealth is all at your command  <BR>If you&#39;ll remove your icy hands  <BR>Oh the young the rich or poor  <BR>All alike to me you know  <BR>No wealth no land no silver or gold  <BR>Nothin satisfies my but your soul  <BR>Oh death  <BR>Oh death  <BR>Won&#39;t you spare me over til another year  <BR>Won&#39;t you spare me over til another year  <BR>Won&#39;t you spare me over til another year  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 955-->
<!--Post: 956--><!--Time: 1093959362-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST956"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beckyks<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beckyks<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, August 31, 2004 - 6:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Maybe he was rhyming &#34;stand&#34; to &#34;hands&#34; <BR> <BR>&#34;I&#39;ll tie your hands till you can&#39;t make a stand.&#34;  <BR> <BR>I realize it&#39;s different phrasing than the rest of the poem, but that&#39;s how I took it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 956-->
<!--Post: 957--><!--Time: 1093971627-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST957"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Colette<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Colette<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, August 31, 2004 - 10:00 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;can&#39;t make a stand&#34; ... can&#39;t get away from him or fight back &#40;ala Custer&#39;s last stand, ect.&#41;You can&#39;t really fight back with your hands tied.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 957-->
<!--Post: 962--><!--Time: 1094093051-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST962"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, September 01, 2004 - 7:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Original “Oh Death” Poem <BR>“Whoa death someone could pray/ Could you wait to call me til another day” <BR>“Oh, Anna” Poem <BR>“Fantasy Spring forth, mounts, to storm fury, then winter clam at the end.” <BR> <BR>These two lines almost sound similar to me. “Whoa” is how you stop a horse. The first part of the Oh Anna line sounds like he’s alluding to a horse &#40;death&#41;, in a “fury,” ready for Anna, but she doesn’t come home.   <BR> <BR>“winter clam at the end.” April is the end of oyster/clam season. Clams and oysters are considered to be aphrodisiacs &#40;internet&#41;. BTK wrote the letter in June. This sort of fits in with my oyster theory, which I can’t find anymore on the message board. Oh Well. <BR> <BR>Just an idea. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 962-->
<!--Post: 972--><!--Time: 1094173396-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST972"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Faithful_reader<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Faithful_reader<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, September 02, 2004 - 6:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I thought &#34;winter clam at the end&#34; was another btk signature typo for &#34;winter calm at the end&#34; This would be a contrast for the fury.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 972-->
<!--Post: 975--><!--Time: 1094183032-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST975"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Faithful_reader<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Faithful_reader<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, September 02, 2004 - 8:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->And the spring.  winter calm/spring fury. sorry to be so disjointed. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 975-->
<!--Post: 981--><!--Time: 1094211455-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST981"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, September 03, 2004 - 4:37 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->True, but I feel BTK reversed letters to make &#34;clam&#34; for a reason.&#34; Also it would be too normal to write calm, sound too much like real poetry. BTK probably wanted to be scary. <BR> <BR>Also, BTK said he wanted to be like known serial killers, Jack the Ripper included. &#34;clam&#34; is an acronym for the Rippers victims: Catherine, Liz, Annie, and Mary, correct order would be Annie, Liz, Catherine, and Mary. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 981-->
<!--Post: 986--><!--Time: 1094240538-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST986"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Screamer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Screamer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, September 03, 2004 - 12:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;ve got to hand it to you Bookworm, that&#39;s interesting about the Ripper victims. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 986-->
<!--Post: 989--><!--Time: 1094329968-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST989"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, September 04, 2004 - 1:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Screamer, <BR>If you want to add the Ripper victim Polly Nichols, then it would be CLAMP which works similar to the clam except a clamp would be an external force and a clam internal &#40;muscles&#41;. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 989-->
<!--Post: 1008--><!--Time: 1094699583-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1008"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, September 08, 2004 - 8:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I thought BTK sketched Nancy face down. This must be a different sketch, than the one with the shoes. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1008-->
<!--Post: 1009--><!--Time: 1094730065-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1009"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, September 09, 2004 - 4:41 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->“Oh – murder!” and poems “Oh Death,” “Oh Death to Nancy,”  and “Oh Anna, Why Didn’t You Appear” <BR> <BR>Elizabeth Prater &#40;lived upstairs from Kelly&#41; inquest statement in Kelly murder: <BR>“As I was turning round I heard a suppressed cry of &#34;Oh - murder!&#34; in a faint voice.” <BR><a href="http://casebook.org/official_documents/inquests/inquest_kelly.html" target=_top>http://casebook.org/official_documents/inquests/in quest_kelly.html</a> <BR> <BR>Elizabeth Prater statement as quoted in Knight’s book  “Final Solution/ Jack the Ripper &#40;1976&#41;” &#40;HB, p. 68&#41; <BR>“…just then I heard screams of murder about two or three times in a female voice.” <BR>Knight does not exactly quote the inquest statement. <BR> <BR>If BTK borrowed the “Oh” from the Mary Kelly case also, he would have had to researched <BR> the original inquest, or a newspaper archive if the quote was published. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1009-->
<!--Post: 1022--><!--Time: 1094872393-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1022"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Boo<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Boo<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->9-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, September 10, 2004 - 8:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think we may &#40;?&#41; be dealing with an Irishman here -- or a man of Irish heritage.  Why? For one thing, the &#39;Anna&#39; poem.  If it &#42;is&#42; indeed original, reread it with that idea in mind.  Is it my vivid imagination working overtime or does it have a very lyrical, Irish-sounding lilt to it?   <BR>Hmmmm... for instance, &#34;T&#39;was perfect plan of deviant pleasure so bold on that spring nite&#34; ...and...&#34;Alone again I trod in pass &#40;past&#41; memory of mirrors, and ponder why for number eight was not&#34;.  Who talks or even writes that way anymore?  Oh, I know, I know...poetic license &amp; all that... but &#34;T&#39;was&#34;?  &#34;I trod&#34;?  &#34;and ponder why for number eight was not&#34;?  <BR> <BR>Also, the Vian murder took place on March 17th &#40;St. Patrick&#39;s Day&#41; and the first letter that emerged out of the woodwork this year, after years of silence, was dated March 17th.  Coincidence?  Perhaps. <BR> <BR>I simply don&#39;t have the time right now to research this -- but if anyone out there does -- and thinks there may be something to this &#39;Irish&#39; idea...it would be interesting to know if any of the other dates of the murders &#42;or&#42; dates of his letters have any Irish ties or correspond with significant Irish holidays.   <BR> <BR>Any or all of these ideas may have been presented on this board before -- and if so? Gimme a break!  I&#39;m a first-time poster and have only been reading it sporadically over the past couple of months as time allows. <BR> <BR>Ciao, <BR>~ Boo<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1022-->
<!--Post: 1025--><!--Time: 1094914987-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1025"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, September 11, 2004 - 8:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;OH, DEATH WHERE IS THY VICTORY? OH, GRAVE WHERE IS THY STING?&#34; St. Paul to the Corinthians. Could it be that BTK reads his Bible? <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s longings to be a poet will never be realized, because of the darkness within him. Poetry arises from sensitivity, joy, and pain. His evil blocks the expressions of those emotions. Hence, he must borrow words and feelings from those whom he envies. <BR> <BR>To quote other poets, BTK has a rendezvous with death. And when he falls, he falls like Lucifer, never to hope again. <BR> <BR>Yes, BTK does appear to have an odd way of pronouncing his &#39;o&#39; &#40;homicide, Fox&#41;. Boris Karloff appeared to have an accent, but he was giving correct pronunciation and enunciation of the English language. I still think that BTK may have had some speech/theater experience. Or, perhaps English is a well learned second language for him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1025-->
<!--Post: 1027--><!--Time: 1095013927-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1027"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Muskogee<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Muskogee<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 12, 2004 - 11:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->IF BTK wrote this poem himself &#40;and I doubt he did&#41;, the emphasis on the natural world is important, in my opinion.  This would lead me to believe BTK is a hiker, birder, or otherwise engages in hobbies associated with the outdoors.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1027-->
<!--Post: 1029--><!--Time: 1095039211-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1029"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Boo<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Boo<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->9-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, September 12, 2004 - 6:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->~ Teardrop... <BR> <BR>Your entry of 8/27... &#34;and the younger woman may have still been the intended victim, with BTK trying again to confuse the issue and the police...&#34;  I agree with you on one count.  I believe BTK thought that &#39;ANNA&#39; was the younger woman &#40;daughter&#41;.  I think BTK may have been the one that was perhaps &#42;confused&#42;.  Possible?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1029-->
<!--Post: 1031--><!--Time: 1095125089-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1031"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Faithful_reader<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Faithful_reader<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, September 13, 2004 - 6:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have been mulling this over for a while, and Teardrop&#39;s mention of an interest in theater confirms or solidifies my idea:  Many actors and actresses spend a great deal of time &#34;in character&#34; and in an effort to walk and talk like their character, will make an effort to think like their character too.  I think BTK is a character that some regular guy &#34;Gets into&#34; when the mood &#40;or need&#41; strikes him.  All the speculation about English not being a first language and possible Spanish influenced syntax---to me that&#39;s who his character is.  All the grammatical stuff has always seemed so contrived to me...and I think this is why.  The same with the poetry....the spelling errors are similar to those someone makes when typing quickly and drops or transposes letters, but aren&#39;t his communications, hand written?  It&#39;s all about the persona he is acting out.  Does this make any sense to anyone else?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1031-->
<!--Post: 1032--><!--Time: 1095159953-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1032"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 14, 2004 - 4:05 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Faithful_reader this may fit into your theory: <BR> <BR>The Otero’s car was parked at a grocery store by the name of Dillon’s &#40;correct me if I’m wrong&#41; and the next murder is of Kathryn Bright. <BR><a href="http://www.tvtome.com/tvtome/servlet/EpisodeGuideServlet/showid-236/Gunsmoke" target=_top>http://www.tvtome.com/tvtome/servlet/EpisodeGuideS ervlet/showid-236/Gunsmoke</a> <BR> <BR>Episode of Gunsmoke a few days before Kathryn Bright’s murder, where Matt Dillon is shot up. This episode is Kitty’s &#40;Kathleen Russell&#41; last episode in the series. <BR>01-Apr-1974     “Disciple” <BR>&#34;Oh Matt,&#34; Kitty&#39;s last words onscreen when Dillon returns to Dodge City.” <BR> <BR>“Oh Matt” compare “Oh Death.” Matt and Wyatt don’t rhyme but are spelled similar. Dodge City is in Kansas. Kevin is shot like Matt Dillon. <BR> <BR>The episode before the Otero murders on January 14, 1974 is called  “A Family of Killers” and since BTK likes to reverse/mirror words/letters, he would be a “Killer of Families.”   Their car was the one parked at Dillon’s. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1032-->
<!--Post: 1033--><!--Time: 1095160957-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1033"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 14, 2004 - 4:22 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.americanwest.com/pages/dodge.htm" target=_top>http://www.americanwest.com/pages/dodge.htm</a> <BR>Wyatt Earp was a Marshall in Dodge City for a while and died on January 13th. &#40;Find a grave link&#41;. <BR> <BR>P.J. Wyatt used the poem &#34;Oh Death&#34; in a class she taught.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1033-->
<!--Post: 1035--><!--Time: 1095164229-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1035"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 14, 2004 - 5:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is a book called FIEND about a boy serial killer &#40;Jesse Pomeroy&#41; who wrote poetry. I believe his writings came after his incarceration. The author is Harold Schechter. Perhaps BTK can pick up some poetic pointers, if he has not already read the book. He seems to have studied these monsters.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1035-->
<!--Post: 1036--><!--Time: 1095188943-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1036"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 14, 2004 - 12:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bookworm, I clicked on the Gunsmoke link you provided and the episode nearest the Otero murders has a partial text of the script or Quotes section. There is mention of a watch, which was one of the items stolen from the Otero home.  <BR>-Deb <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1036-->
<!--Post: 1040--><!--Time: 1095283714-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1040"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, September 15, 2004 - 2:28 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Flytodeb, <BR>I can never remember all the details of the crimes. I have to look them up all the time. Thank you for noticing that. <BR> <BR>The Brights were family as were the Oteros. BTK probably saw the titles of the episodes in the TV Guide for that week. I don&#39;t know if they would have had episode previews back then. <BR> <BR>I know the store on that corner is Dillons now, but didn&#39;t know if that store existed at the time of the Otero murders.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1040-->
<!--Post: 1051--><!--Time: 1095815818-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1051"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, September 21, 2004 - 6:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->PROPENSION - This is a French word, though it has an English counterpart. Another tail chasing party, sponsored by BTK. He may be cunning but he cannot come up with anything original, not in poetry or serial killing. He is a wanna-be and his poetry sucks eggs. His only claim to fame is a secret that he must keep. <BR> <BR>Should he die without being id&#39;d, I would speculate that somewhere he has a safety deposit box filled with enough mementos to ensure his final identification as BTK.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1051-->
<!--Post: 1138--><!--Time: 1097421138-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1138"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wolfgang<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wolfgang<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, October 10, 2004 - 8:12 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->A deposit box Teardrop ?, no he won&#39;t, he will <BR>want the &#39;mementos&#39; close at hand to re-live <BR>the moments.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1138-->
<!--Post: 1145--><!--Time: 1097448344-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1145"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Verm_inve<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Verm_inve<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, October 10, 2004 - 3:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Crimelibrary.com has made a connection with the &#34;anna&#34; poem to a poem by famed James Joyce.  You can read about it in the newest chapter on the BTK at their site. <BR> <BR>They had made a very interesting suggestion about the &#34;T&#39; &#34; at the beginning of the poem.  Very cleaver if it holds true.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1145-->
<!--Post: 1198--><!--Time: 1097674797-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1198"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 13, 2004 - 6:39 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here&#39;s some more interesting information about the Joyce poem which was itself based on an old 1850&#39;s song. <BR> <BR>&#34;Joyce removed the apostrophe in the title to assert an active process in which a multiplicity of &#34;Finnegans,&#34; that is, all of us, wake, that is, arise after falling.&#34; <BR> <BR><a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnegan" target=_top>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnegan</a>&#39;s_Wake <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1198-->
<!--Post: 1199--><!--Time: 1097694493-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1199"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 13, 2004 - 12:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OK, maybe its been discussed but I can&#39;t find it in search.  Tom&#39;s informant says that an FBI profile said that BTK went to West Point.  There was an old poem at West Point entitled &#34;Oh, Anna&#34; that all the underclassmen knew. It dealt with analytical geometry, a very hard class in the old days. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1199-->
<!--Post: 1200--><!--Time: 1097698669-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1200"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 13, 2004 - 1:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sorry about the link to Finnegan&#39;s Wake. Here&#39;s a repost. It has the song lyric. <BR><a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnegan%27s_Wake" target=_top>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnegan%27s_Wake</a> <BR> <BR>Warren do you have the lyrics?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1200-->
<!--Post: 1201--><!--Time: 1097701826-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1201"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 13, 2004 - 2:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;m looking Book.  I&#39;ve seen it before.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1201-->
<!--Post: 1208--><!--Time: 1097772474-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1208"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, October 14, 2004 - 9:47 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here you go, Book: <BR> <BR>A SONNET TO &#34;ANALYTICAL.&#34; <BR> <BR>Of all the girls I ever knew, <BR>    The one I&#39;ve most neglected, <BR>Is called Miss &#34;Anna Lytical,&#34; <BR>    For her I&#39;ve least respected. <BR> <BR>Oh! Anna! Anna Lytical <BR>    I&#39;ll never love you more, <BR>For you, I fear, will cause my fall, <BR>    And make me leave the Corps. <BR> <BR>And now, -- oh! Anna Lytical! <BR>    I hope that &#34;Albert E.,&#34; <BR>Before the &#34;Board,&#34; will not give out <BR>    A word of you to me. <BR> <BR>For if he does, now Anna, <BR>    I tremble at the sound, <BR>For my examination would  <BR>    Result in being &#34;found.&#34; <BR> <BR>Oh! cruel Anna Lytical! <BR>    With truth I now can say, <BR>I fear you&#39;ll never let me see <BR>    My &#34;Graduation day.&#34; <BR> <BR>&#40;This is from the mid to late 1800&#39;s at West Point.&#41; <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1208-->
<!--Post: 1223--><!--Time: 1097838791-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1223"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 15, 2004 - 4:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks Warren, <BR>That is interesting. Seems that BTK liked vintage poetry/songs if he used these as a resource for his letters. <BR> <BR>If he went to West Point, he had money, brains, or both.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1223-->
<!--Post: 1237--><!--Time: 1097853977-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1237"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 15, 2004 - 8:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->...or connections.  I think BTK&#39;s poetry was influenced by Bayord Taylor and George Cabot Lodge.  However, the more I look at &#34;Oh, Anna&#34;, the more I see a poem that was translated into English.  Originally in Spanish?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1237-->
<!--Post: 1248--><!--Time: 1097901956-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1248"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 15, 2004 - 9:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Are you talking about the West Point &#34;Oh Anna,&#34; Warren, being translated? <BR> <BR>Just my opinion but I think the Anna victim/poem is another &#40;Anne&#41;Lindbergh connection for whatever reason. Maybe BTK didn&#39;t like the Lindberghs? <BR> <BR>The &#34;Oh Anna&#34; Poem was received on June 15th, exactly a week before Anne Lindbergh&#39;s 73rd birthday. &#40;Internet reference&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1248-->
<!--Post: 1250--><!--Time: 1097962025-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1250"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, October 16, 2004 - 2:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->No, Book. I think the way BTK&#39;s Oh Anna reads it is very disjointed.  If its original, then I think it shows a disjointed personality.  But to me, it looks like he took a non-English poem and translated it into English.  I think some of his translated words are not the best words to convey the non-English meaning.  For example &#34;enrapture garments&#34;..I bet there is a better translation.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1250-->
<!--Post: 1251--><!--Time: 1097969526-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1251"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dpinva<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dpinva<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, October 16, 2004 - 4:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is a Spanish translation of the Anna poem from the EZBoard BTK site. &#40;Thanks to TheMoodyBlue for his translation of it&#41; <BR> <BR> <BR>¿Oh, Ana Porqué Le Hizo Para No Aparecer? <BR>&#40;Oh, Anna, Why Didn&#39;t You Appear&#41;  <BR> <BR>Era plan perfecto del placer irregular;  <BR>tan en negrilla en ese nite del resorte. <BR>Mi sensación interna caliente con el propension;  <BR>de la nueva estación que despierta. <BR>Caliente, mojado con miedo y éxtasis internos; <BR>Mi placer del enredo.  <BR>Como vides nuevas en la noche;  <BR>¿Oh, Ana Porqué Le hizo Para no aparecer? <BR> <BR>Gota del miedo, lluvia fresca del resorte; Rodaría abajo de su nakedness.  <BR>Para sospechar a la fiebre alta, esa quemaduras dentro;  <BR>En ese mundo pequeño de longing, de miedo, del éxtasis, y de la desesperación. <BR>El juego que jugamos, que nos caemos en los oídos del diablo;  <BR>Resorte de la fantasía adelante, montajes a la furia de la tormenta;  <BR>Entonces calma del invierno en el extremo.  <BR>¿Oh, Ana Porqué Le hizo Para no aparecer? <BR> <BR>Solamente, ahora en otra duración;  <BR>Pongo con enrapture dulce;  <BR>ropa a través de la mayoría del pensamiento privado. <BR>Cama de la hierba húmeda del resorte;  <BR>limpie antes del sol;  <BR>esclavizado con control;  <BR>viento caliente que sospecha el aire;  <BR>rasgones de la chispa de la luz del sol;  <BR>en los ojos tan profundos y claros. <BR>Solamente pisé otra vez;  <BR>en la memoria del paso de espejos;  <BR>y ponder porqué;  <BR>Para el número ocho no estaban.  <BR>¿Oh, Ana Porqué Le hizo Para no aparecer? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1251-->
<!--Post: 1257--><!--Time: 1098064234-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1257"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Warren_b<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Warren_b<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, October 17, 2004 - 6:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Damn, Dpinva, that&#39;s good.  This is beginning to make sense. It does flow better in Spanish. Would this classify as a sonnet?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1257-->
<!--Post: 1285--><!--Time: 1098826391-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1285"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lisa<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lisa<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, October 26, 2004 - 2:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Warren, some do think that BTK is not English because of his accent.  Perhaps that poem in Spanish confirms this?  The poem in Spanish does sound better. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1285-->
